.TH PATHCHECK 1 "September 2026" "pathcheck 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pathcheck \- explain command lookup through PATH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pathcheck
.I program
.SH DESCRIPTION
Supply exactly one nonempty command name without a slash.
Invalid arguments produce a diagnostic on standard error and exit status 2.
.PP
.B pathcheck
examines each directory in the PATH environment variable for the named program.
It reports executable matches, non-executable files, directories, and the first executable candidate that would be selected by PATH order.
.PP
Only regular files that pass the executable permission check qualify.
Symlinks are followed; dangling links are ordinary misses.
Directories and special files such as FIFOs and devices are never selected.
Later qualifying candidates are reported as shadowed, including repeated paths.
.PP
An empty PATH component is treated as the current directory, including an
entirely empty PATH and leading, trailing, or consecutive colons.
Relative entries are resolved from the current directory.
An unset PATH produces a diagnostic on standard error and status 1.
.PP
Missing candidates and non-directory path components are ordinary misses.
A failed executable permission check is reported as non-executable.
Other inspection failures are reported on standard error with the candidate
path and system error. The search continues, but any inspection error makes
the final status 1, even if an executable candidate is found.
Output then ends with "lookup incomplete: inspection errors occurred".
Selected and shadowed labels describe only successfully inspected candidates.
.SH PATH DIAGNOSTICS
Entry warnings appear on standard output after the associated candidate and
selection label. Entry numbers follow the original PATH order.
For example:
.PP
.nf
      warning: PATH[3] duplicates PATH[1]
      warning: PATH[4] directory missing
      warning: PATH[5] not a directory
      warning: PATH[6] empty entry searches current directory
      warning: PATH[7] relative entry
      warning: PATH[8] world-writable directory (sticky bit set)
.fi
.PP
Duplicates reference the first entry with exactly the same component text.
Repeated empty components count as duplicates; an empty component and "." do not.
Trailing slashes, alternate spellings, and symlink aliases are not normalized.
Every entry is still searched in order.
.PP
Missing directories, non-directory entries, and non-directory intermediate
components produce warnings. Warnings alone do not change lookup status.
Other directory inspection failures produce a standard-error diagnostic and
status 1, even when a candidate is found.
.PP
Empty components are labeled as current-directory searches.
Nonempty components not starting with "/" are labeled relative, including "."
and "./bin". These labels describe the original text even for missing directories.
.PP
Directories with the other-write mode bit set are labeled world-writable.
The warning states whether the sticky bit is set and remains in either case.
The sticky bit restricts removal and renaming of directory entries; it does
not prevent new entries or make command lookup safe.
Symlinks are followed, so warnings describe the target directory.
These mode-bit observations are not a security audit: ACLs, parent-directory
permissions, mount options, and concurrent filesystem changes are not evaluated.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
An executable candidate was found and no inspection error occurred.
.TP
.B 1
No executable candidate was found, or a runtime error occurred.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.
.SH NOTES
Only filesystem candidates are inspected; shell aliases, functions, builtins,
and cached command locations are not resolved.
An executable permission check does not guarantee successful execution.
.PP
The implementation uses ANSI C / C89 language features plus POSIX filesystem interfaces such as stat(2) and access(2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR execvp (3),
.BR environ (7)
